--- !u!114 &749161375
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 749161373}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.TextMeshPro::TMPro.TextMeshProUGUI
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "<size=140%><b>\uD55C\uAE00\uB180\uC774 \u2014 Hangulnoree</b></size>\r\n\r\nBuild
    real Korean words from their smallest pieces. No prior Korean needed.\r\n\r\n\r\n<size=120%><b>The
    Korean alphabet</b></size>\r\n\r\nKorean is written in <b>Hangul</b> (\uD55C\uAE00).
    Its letters are called <b>jamo</b> (\uC790\uBAA8), and there are only 24 basic
    ones \u2014 you can learn the whole set.\r\n\r\nUnlike English, jamo isn't written
    in a straight line. They stack into square <b>syllable blocks</b>.\r\n\r\n\r\n<size=120%><b>Building
    a syllable</b></size>\r\n\r\nEvery syllable block has two or three parts, always
    in this order:\r\n\r\n<b>1. \uCD08\uC131 (choseong)</b> \u2014 a starting consonant\r\n<b>2.
    \uC911\uC131 (jungseong)</b> \u2014 a vowel\r\n<b>3. \uC885\uC131 (jongseong)</b>
    \u2014 an ending consonant (optional)\r\n"
